Each `$before` or `$after` takes one compatible compile-time operand. In a
String construction, use a String or a constant template. In a sequence
construction the prefix and suffix contribute compatible elements. The wrapper
applies once to each direct child construction in prefix, child, suffix order.
Literal text in the parent body and nested children inside that child do not
get a separate wrapper.

Only named slots can occur in a prefix or suffix, including inside nested
templates. The child insertion point is implicit. A named slot in a prefix
or suffix takes its contributions from the enclosing application. Its own
default applies when the caller supplies nothing.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    open_row #= [$html:<tr class="[$slot row_class = "normal"]">]
    rows #= [$before open_row, $after "</tr>":[$slot]]
    result = [rows:[$in row_class:summary][:<td>Total</td>]]
]

The `row_class` insert fills the prefix. The direct child supplies the cell,
so the result is `<tr class="summary"><td>Total</td></tr>`.

A false condition, absent option or zero-iteration loop has no output to wrap.
A selected empty String or sequence gets its prefix and suffix. A splice or a
loop counts as one direct child even when it contributes several items. Move
the wrapper inside the loop body to wrap each iteration.

`$fresh` takes no operand. It skips one immediate parent's child wrapper.
Siblings and wrappers declared inside that child retain their usual behaviour.
These rules also apply to compiled constants, runtime constructions and
wrappers imported through a facade. See @../templates/#child-wrappers (Child
wrappers) for more examples.
